postfixT -- Tony, 2026-11-12 (SEQ 635): x++ and x-- may be written after the operand. No C++ difference: the postfix form is the
prefix form written after, so it increments and yields the NEW value. Rows prefixed PX-. Interpreted only.

One process, three lines a row, as prefixNestT: "old" an incant/grammar action (interpretXPaccum's postfixToo arm), "top" STARt at
top level on kantGrammar, "act" an action body built through kantGrammar (PX RP proves that road). Each line prints r then qv; qv is
reset to 3 and r to "pre" before each. The mark is postfixToo on ++ and -- in incant/setup, read by both roads.
The rule: after an operand, ++ or -- followed by NOTHING OR AN OPERATOR is a postfix on that operand. Followed by an operand it keeps
the old reading, which is a binary ++ refused by name at run time: PX-K1 ("n" ++qv in a print list, old road) pins that one refusal on stderr.
PX-6: a prefix wraps the postfix instruct, - qv++ is -(qv++). PX-7 is the prefix control. PX-9: a postfix before a comparison.
H7 (2026-11-12): with the postfixToo mark removed from setup, PX-1 to PX-6, PX-8 and PX-9 go red on all three columns.

